 pattern (6 points) applies.\n  - Half Flush? No \u2014 you have both honors and suited tiles from multiple suits.\n  - All Pungs? No \u2014 you have two chows.\n  - You have winds: F2 (South Wind) as a pung. Prevailing Wind is South (F2), Seat Wind is East (F1). So you have **Prevalent Wind** (2 points).\n  - You lack self-drawn or fully concealed conditions.\n  - If you win off a discard, you can get **Melded Hand** (6 points), **Prevalent Wind** (2 points), possibly **Dragon Pung**? No dragons.\n  - Possibly **All Chows**? No \u2014 you have two pungs.\n  - Actually, your hand consists of two chows and two pungs \u2014 not qualifying for All Chows.\n\nWait type analysis:\n- To complete the hand, you need to pair either T9 or B2 after discarding the other.\n- Suppose you keep T9: then your pair candidate is T9. Is T9 safe? Three remain unseen. But T9 hasn\u2019t been discarded much \u2014 only one T9 shown? Likely others are still in wall or hands. Possible wait.\n- Keep B2: only 2 B2s left, but previous player discarded B2 at least twice (Play B2), indicating they might be getting rid of it, so possibly safe to keep.\n\nBut crucially: **you cannot win unless you discard one tile and complete a legal 14-tile winning hand with the other as the pair**.\n\nThus, the key is safety and maximizing potential fan.\n\nLooking deeper:\n- If you discard **B2**, you are aiming for **T9 pair**. Are there any indications someone is waiting on B2?\n  - Previous player had two sets of B1-B2-B3 chows \u2014 they may have used up their need for B2. They even played B2 recently. Risk is low.\n- If you discard **T9**, you aim for **B2 pair**. But B2 appears more frequently in others\u2019 melds (next player has B3-B4-B5, B4-B5-B6; across has nothing; previous has strong bamboo focus). B2 is part of B1-B2-B3 which previous completed twice \u2014 so they may still want B2 for pair or kong? Unlikely, since they would have kept duplicates.\n\nHowever, **Unshown tiles show B2: 2 remaining**, meaning two are still available \u2014 not yet exhausted.\n\nBut critically: **you must choose the safer discard to avoid feeding a win**.\n\nNow check what others might be waiting for:\n- Next player has melds: [B3,B4,B5], [W1,W2,W3], [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 focused on middle bamboos and characters. Not likely waiting on B2 or T9.\n- Across: [T2,T3,T4], [T7,T7,T7], [F3,F3,F3] \u2014 strong in T7 and F3. May be going for All Honors or Terminal? But discarded many non-honor tiles. Probably not.\n- Previous: [B5,B6,B7], [B1,B2,B3]\u00d72 \u2014 very bamboo-heavy, especially low-mid range. Already used B1,B2,B3 twice. Could they be going for Seven Pairs in bamboo? Possible, but they\u2019ve discarded B1 and B2 \u2014 unlikely to collect extra copies.\n\nSo both B2 and T9 seem relatively safe.\n\nBut now evaluate strategic value:\n- Keeping **T9** gives you a terminal pair possibility. T9 is less useful for chows except in T7-T8-T9 or T8-T9-B?, which is rare.\n- Keeping **B2** allows a possible B1-B2-B3 chow completion, but you already have no such incomplete sequence.\n\nActually, neither tile contributes to a meld \u2014 both are singleton candidates for pairing.\n\nBut note: **your hand is already complete once you form a pair** \u2014 you just need to discard one and wait for the other to be drawn or claimed.\n\nTherefore, the better move is to discard the tile that is **less likely to complete an opponent\u2019s hand**.\n\nBetween B2 and T9:\n- B2 is part of B1-B2-B3, which the previous player has already made **twice** \u2014 highly likely they are building around that area. Even though they played B2, it might be because they already have enough. Still, **danger remains higher for B2**.\n- T9: no player has shown interest in high dots or bamboos. T9 only useful in T7-T8-T9. Across player has T7s, but no sign of T8/T9. They discarded T8 earlier. So T9 seems safer.\n\nMoreover, unshown T9: 3 remaining \u2014 high supply, so less likely to be a winning tile for others.\n\nHence, **discard B2**, keep T9.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding B2 is slightly riskier due to previous player's heavy bamboo involvement, including two uses of B1-B2-B3. Although they've discarded B2, residual risk exists. Conversely, T9 is a terminal tile with no evidence of opponents building toward it. Keeping T9 offers a safer wait and completes a balanced hand. Final decision favors discarding **B2** only because keeping a terminal like T9 as a pair is acceptable, and B2 is more integrated into active chows.\n\nWait \u2014 valid actions are **[Play B2; Play T9]** \u2014 meaning you must play one.\n\nRechecking: do we have any pattern opportunities?\n\nYou have:\n- Two Pungs: T8s (suit), F2s (Prevailing Wind)\n- Two Chows: T4-T5-T6, W7-W8-W9\n\u2192 This is a mixed hand.\n\nNo chance for flush, all pungs, etc.\n\nBest scoring path: aim for **Melded Hand** (6 points) + **Prevalent Wind** (2 points) = 8 points minimum \u2014 barely qualifies.\n\nTo get **Melded Hand**, all melds must be formed by other players' discards (which they were: Chi T5, Peng T8, Chi W8, Peng F2), and the final pair must be completed by an opponent\u2019s discard.\n\nSo you must **not draw the winning tile yourself** \u2014 but rather claim it.\n\nThus, you should discard the less dangerous tile and wait.\n\nBetween B2 and T9:\n- B2: connected to B1-B2-B3 \u2014 used by previous player in two melds. High interaction. Dangerous.\n- T9: isolated. Only possible in T7-T8-T9. Across player has T7s, but discarded T8 and didn\u2019t pick up T9 \u2014 likely not completing it. Safer.\n\nTherefore, **discard B2**, keep T9 as pair candidate.\n\nAnswer: Play B2\n\nFinal confirmation:\n- After discarding B2, hand becomes: T9 (pair candidate), with four melds.\n- Wait on T9 \u2014 if someone discards it, you can win with 8+ points (Melded Hand + Prevalent Wind).\n- Avoid discarding T9 because it's safer to keep and harder for others to use.\n\nYes.\n\nEvaluation: Both B2 and T9 are singletons needing to be discarded, but B2 is more dangerous due to previous player's repeated use of B1-B2-B3 chows. T9 is a terminal with less utility and lower risk. Keeping T9 as a pair is safer.  \nReason: Discarding B2 minimizes the risk of feeding an opponent's potential wait, especially given the previous player's bamboo-oriented melds, while keeping T9 offers a safer and viable pair for completing the hand under the Melded Hand and Prevalent Wind scoring patterns.  \nAnswer: Play B2"}
